.TH PEKIT 1 2026-10-22 "Peios pekit 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
pekit \- build Peios packages from GCSL recipes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pekit init
.RI [ dir ]
.br
.B pekit package
.RB [ \-\-rebuild ]
.RB [ \-\-overwrite ]
.RB [ \-\-workspace
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-artifact\-dir
.IR dir ]
.IR name ...
.br
.B pekit release
.RB [ \-\-recipe
.IR dir ]...\&
.RB [ \-\-workspace
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-artifact\-dir
.IR dir ]
.RI [ name ...]
.br
.B pekit
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help | \-V | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pekit
builds Peios packages from recipes written in GCSL and packs them with
.BR ppk (1).
.PP
A recipe workspace is a GCSL workspace: a directory tree whose root holds
.IR workspace.gcsl .
Its root also holds
.IR pekit\-include.gcsl ,
pekit's declarations of the definition types recipes use, which
.B pekit init
writes.
Every directory below the root sees them without importing them, and so
does a GCSL language server, which reads the file like any other.
.I workspace.gcsl
is an ordinary GCSL file that belongs to the workspace's author.
It can give pekit's settings for the workspace as a field named
.BR pekitconf ,
of the type
.BR PekitConfig :
.RS
.PP
.EX
pekitconf: PekitConfig = { artifact_dir = "_artifacts" }
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B artifact_dir
is the directory pekit packs packages into, relative to the workspace's
root.
.PP
A recipe defines three kinds of definition:
.TP
.B source
An upstream source: a
.B url
and the
.B sha256
of what it serves, or a
.B local_path
to a source tree on this machine, relative to the workspace's root unless
absolute.
A build is given a copy of a local tree: in a Git work tree, of the files
Git would commit, as they are on disk, so that uncommitted changes are built
and ignored files such as build output are not; anywhere else, of all of it.
.TP
.B build
The steps that make an install tree, laid out as
.IR /usr ,
from the unpacked source.
A
.B defmeta
gives the build's
.B source
and its
.BR dependencies .
.TP
.B package
A package: its
.BR build ,
the
.B files
it takes from the build's install tree, and its runtime
.BR dependencies .
.PP
A recipe file also gives
.BR "pekit = 1" ,
the recipe format it is written for, and the
.B version
and
.B revision
of its packages, which make the package's version:
.B version
.BR "= \(dq1.37.0\(dq" ,
.B revision
.B "= \(dq1\(dq"
makes 1.37.0\-1.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR init " [\fIdir\fR]"
Write
.I pekit\-include.gcsl
into the workspace at
.IR dir ,
the current directory by default, replacing any there, and an empty
.I workspace.gcsl
if there is none.
An existing
.I workspace.gcsl
is left as it is.
.TP
.BI package " name\fR..."
Build each package
.I name
and pack it into the artifact directory.
On standard output,
.B pekit package
prints one line for each package, in the order given: its
.IR name @ version
and its identity.
If a package's version is already packed for the host's architecture, it
prints that identity and does not build the package again, unless
.B \-\-overwrite
is given.
Packages that share a build run it once.
.IP
Otherwise
.B pekit package
creates the artifact directory if it is missing and checks that it can
write there, then gets the install tree of the package's build, from its
cache or by running the build.
It copies the files the package chooses from the install tree and packs
them as
.IR name @ version ,
for the host's architecture.
.IP
The cache keeps each install tree a build returns under a key made of
everything pekit gives the build: the files of the recipe the build is in
and those it imports, the source's content, its
.BR SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH ,
the architecture, and pekit's version.
A build whose key has a tree kept does not run.
The key cannot see what the build uses from the host, such as its
compiler; when that changes, use
.BR \-\-rebuild .
.IP
To run a build,
.B pekit package
fetches the source into its cache and checks its SHA-256, unless the cache
holds it already, and unpacks it; a local source is read where it is.
Each build has a work directory that is kept between runs.
It holds the build's copy of its source, which is synced from the source
before each run: a file is rewritten only if its content or execute bit
changed, or the build changed it, so every other file keeps its
modification time, and a file is deleted only if the last sync placed it
and the source no longer has it.
What a build makes in its copy, or beside the copy, is kept, so that a
build that redoes only what changed, such as one run by
.BR make (1),
is quick after a small change.
The build runs with its copy of the source as
.BR source_path ,
an empty directory in its work directory as
.BR out_path ,
and the host's architecture as
.BR arch .
A run of the build is locked against another run of the same build.
.IP
Each entry of
.B files
maps a path in
.I /usr
to the path in the install tree it comes from.
Both are relative,
.BR / \-separated,
and have no
.B .\&
or
.B ..\&
segments.
A last segment of
.B *
on both sides takes every entry of a directory.
A directory is taken whole, and a symbolic link as it is, never followed.
.IP
The build runs on the host, with the host's tools, and only the
.B sh
calls of its steps reach outside GCSL.
Build and runtime dependencies are not supported yet: a recipe that gives
any is refused.
.TP
.BR release " [\fIname\fR...]"
Build and pack each package
.IR name ,
and every package the recipe in each
.B \-\-recipe
directory defines, then tag the commit that made them.
The workspace must be in a Git repository, with no uncommitted changes in
the workspace, untracked files included; files Git ignores do not count.
A package whose build has a
.B local_path
source cannot be released, because a tag cannot pin what the tree holds.
.IP
Each package is tagged
.IR name @ version ,
such as
.BR net.busybox.busybox@1.37.0\-1 ,
at
.BR HEAD .
The tag does not include the architecture: the commit makes the version for
every architecture, though
.B pekit release
builds only the host's.
.IP
Every package is built, even one already packed, and packed only if it
matches what is packed already.
Builds do not use the cache: each runs in a new work directory, which is
removed afterwards, so what is released comes from the source and recipe
alone.
A release therefore succeeds again for a version already released, and
fails if the recipe now makes something different without a new
.BR revision .
Packages that share a build run it once.
.IP
Tags are only added.
They are made only after every package is packed, all of them or none,
and a tag that already exists is kept, even at an earlier commit, as the
first commit that made its package.
The tags are made in the local repository only; push them with
.BR git (1).
.IP
On standard output,
.B pekit release
prints one line for each package: its tag and its identity.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-rebuild
For
.BR package :
run each build afresh, ignoring any install tree the cache keeps for it,
from an empty work directory, and keep the tree it returns in place of the
old one.
A package whose version is already packed is still not built again.
.TP
.B \-\-overwrite
For
.BR package :
build each package even if its version is already packed, and pack it in
place of what is packed, saying so if it differs.
Only the version's ref changes: the manifest and blobs it named stay in
the artifact directory.
A packed version is meant never to change, so this is for development,
such as packing a package built from a local source after a change, into
an artifact directory nothing else relies on.
It can be combined with
.BR \-\-rebuild .
.TP
.BI \-\-recipe " dir"
For
.BR release :
release every package the recipe in
.I dir
defines.
.I dir
is relative to the workspace's root, such as
.BR net.busybox.busybox .
It can be given more than once, and with package names.
.TP
.BI \-\-workspace " dir"
Read recipes from the workspace at
.IR dir ,
rather than the workspace the current directory is in.
.TP
.BI \-\-artifact\-dir " dir"
Store packages in
.IR dir ,
rather than the directory the workspace's
.B pekitconf
gives, or
.I /system/artifacts
when it gives none, as
.BR ppk (1)
does.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including printing help or the version.
.TP
.B 1
Failure, such as a workspace whose include file is not this pekit's, a
recipe with errors, an artifact directory pekit cannot write to, a
release from a workspace with uncommitted changes, a version that cannot
be a Git tag's name, a source whose SHA-256 differs from the recipe's, a
build that fails, or a package version that is already packed with
different contents.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: an unknown option or a missing argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
The base of pekit's cache,
.I ~/.cache
when it is unset or empty.
.TP
.B SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
Set by
.B pekit package
for the build, replacing any value it is given, to the modification time
of the newest file in the source, so that tools that stamp a time into
what they make give the same result each time.
A local source's time changes when a file is touched, even if its content
does not, and the build then runs again.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR $XDG_CACHE_HOME /peios/pekit/sources/sha256/ hash
A fetched source, named by its SHA-256.
.TP
.IR $XDG_CACHE_HOME /peios/pekit/builds/ key
An install tree a build returned, by its key.
.TP
.IR $XDG_CACHE_HOME /peios/pekit/work/ workspace / build / arch /
The work directory of
.I build
of a workspace, named by a hash of the workspace's path, for
.IR arch .
.I source
is the build's copy of its source and
.I out
its
.BR out_path ;
files named
.IR pekit\-* ,
the record of the last sync among them, are pekit's.
.TP
.IR $XDG_CACHE_HOME /peios/pekit/work/build\- *
A release's work directory, removed when the build is done.
.PP
Nothing in the cache is removed automatically.
Removing any of it is safe when pekit is not running: a build whose tree
is gone runs again, and a build whose work directory is gone runs from
nothing.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B pekit init
Make the current directory a recipe workspace, or bring its
.I pekit\-include.gcsl
up to date.
.TP
.B pekit package net.busybox.busybox \-\-artifact\-dir artifacts
Build busybox from its recipe and pack it into
.IR artifacts ,
whatever the workspace gives.
.TP
.B pekit release \-\-recipe net.busybox.busybox
Build and pack every package of the busybox recipe and tag each at
.BR HEAD .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ppk (1),
.BR gcsl (1),
.BR git (1)
